    "explanation": "To both parties. This trip deviated, and the refund is the arithmetic one.\n\nTHE DEVIATION. POL-RD-01 measures the excess on gps_trace.trace_distance_km, which is 23.97 km, against the booked optimal of 18.0 km — an excess of 5.97 km, or 33.2%. The 24.0 km on the trip record is the rounded convenience copy of the same measurement and is not the figure the threshold test uses. Both establishment gates are passed (over 20% and over 0.8 km), so the deviation is established. The excess reaches neither materiality gate (50% or 8.0 km), so this is not a MATERIAL deviation and POL-RD-06 is not engaged: there is no repeat-deviation uplift and no driver-conduct referral. The rider's profile does not change this either — deviation_claims_60d is 1, below the threshold of 4, so no corroboration beyond the trace is required. The trace is clean for policy purposes under POL-RD-07 (241 fixes at a 10 s interval, 100% coverage, no gap over 90 s, no jump over 500 m), so the deviation needs no corroboration; in any event the fare ledger's off-optimal Adam Road gantry independently places the vehicle off the optimal corridor, and the driver's chat message — 'AYE jam boss, this way faster' — admits the reroute.\n\nNO EXCUSE RUNS. POL-RD-04 requires a rider instruction and the only rider message is a complaint about the route, not a request; waypoint_hint is null, so that test cannot be run. POL-RD-05 and POL-RD-12 do not apply (no endpoint was amended; the vehicle is RYDE_X, not RYDE_POOL). POL-RD-03 requires a traffic advisory and there is none. The driver's account that the AYE was crawling is a general assertion that traffic was bad, and POL-RD-08 is explicit that such an assertion, unsupported by a traffic_advisory ID, does not discharge the driver's burden. I do NOT escalate on the empty advisory (EV-ADVIS-00114-01, no payload, weight 0.0). POL-RD-10(5) fires where an exculpatory clause is invoked against a CITED artifact that is missing; here the driver cites no evidence at all and his own statement records 'No advisory that I know of.' The advisory's absence is itself citable and defeats the excuse on the merits; it is not a gap that stops the case being decided on a complete, unimpaired trace.\n\nTHE MONEY. The fare is METERED, so POL-RD-02 runs. recomputed_fare = ((3.80 + 0.75 x 18.0 + 0.28 x 30.0) x 1.6) + 0.80 platform fee + 2.50 on-route ERP = 44.42. The 1.6 multiplier is read from fare_breakdown.surge_multiplier and is preserved on the optimal route — the rider accepts the surge and the recomputation charges it. charged_total is 58.10. The refund is 58.10 - 44.42 = 13.68, rounded UP to SGD 13.70. That is 23.6% of the charge, well short of the 90% that would convert it to a full refund and well above the 0.50 de minimis, so the outcome is PARTIAL_REFUND. The $2.00 Adam Road ERP line is itemised for transparency but is already comprehended in that figure and is never added on top (POL-RD-09(1)); both charged gantries are matched to the trace within the 250 m radius (0.0 m and 8.76 m), so neither is a phantom line and no billing-integrity referral arises. No driver compensation is due: the distance billed (24.0 km) is not short of the trace.\n\nIn short: the rider's 'extra 6 km' is 5.97 km on the measurement the clause governs, and the recoverable sum is SGD 13.70 — not the 15.00 contested, and not the 15.70 that would double-count the off-route ERP."
